ic cycles of the data and the geometry of L and H. The question \"how do period settings affect performance\" therefore becomes: characterize the error-vs-P curve per horizon and per lookback, isolate it from seed noise, and test whether cycle alignment (vs. window divisibility/capacity effects) explains the optimum. Because the repo and csv are absent, every design element that depends on an unverifiable detail is written as an IF/THEN branch.\n\n## 2. Pre-flight verification protocol (mandatory, before any interpretation)\n- **V1 Data verification**: load ETTm1 csv; compute median timestamp delta \u2192 actual sampling interval `s` (min); set nominal daily cycle `C = 1440/s` (expected 144 under A2). Confirm `C \u2248 144` independently via ACF/FFT on the target column on the train split only. All \"cycle alignment\" claims require V1 to pass; otherwise re-center grids on the verified `C` and note the deviation.\n- **V2 Period semantics**: run one forward pass at two distinct period values; inspect intermediate shapes to record whether \"period\" means sub-series **length** (tokens = L/P) or sub-series **count** (tokens = P). If count-convention, the sweep grids below are applied via the mapping count \u2194 L/P and results relabeled to length for comparability.\n- **V3 Divisibility vs padding**: smoke-test a non-divisor period (P=144 with L=512). If accepted (pad/truncate), the \"padding branch\" is open; if it errors, only divisor grids are run.\n- **V4 period vs period_list**: record whether the repo exposes a single `period` or a `period_list` (+ discovery). Sweep rule: if period_list, fix list cardinality to 1 (`[P]`) for the sweep; if cardinality > 1 is forced by code, sweep the dominant element holding all other elements at repo defaults, and label this deviation. The discovery-vs-sweep comparison (\u00a76 D5) is run only if discovery exists.\n- **V5 Environment pinning**: record repo git hash, full dependency lockfile, target column name (expect `OT`, A7), and split border counts actually used; these go verbatim into the results file.\n\n## 3. Sweep table (conditions vs. controls)\n**Varying (the only intended sources of variance):** (a) period P, (b) horizon H, (c) seq_len condition (primary/secondary), (d) random seed.\n\n| Condition | seq_len (L) | Period grid | H | Seeds | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary sweep | 96 | **{8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 96}** (all divisors of 96; valid under V3 either way; P=96 doubles as the degenerate single-sub-series control; tokens L/P = 12\u20261) | {96, 192, 336, 720} | 3 shared | 84 |\n| Secondary (512, long-window branch; only place the daily cycle is resolvable) | 512 | **{32, 64, 128, 256, 512}** (divisors) + **{144, 288} only if V3 padding branch**, else 128/256/512 interpreted as bracketing the nominal 144 per V1 | {96, 192, 336, 720} | 3 shared | 60 (+24) |\n| Discovery comparison (IF V4 discovery exists) | 96 & 512 | repo-auto-discovered | {96, 192, 336, 720} | 3 shared | 24 |\n| Seed-noise control (2 duplicate cells, new seeds 4\u20135) | 96 | 2 grid points | 96 | 2 extra | 4 |\n| Total | | | | | **168\u2013196** |\n\n**Held fixed (controls):** ETTm1 file and split; preprocessing (train-fitted standardization, no leakage); backbone architecture and *all* its hyperparameters (d_model, heads, layers, dropout, factor, activation) at repo PDF/ETTm1 defaults; optimizer, learning rate and schedule, batch size, max epochs, early-stopping patience and monitored metric; target column; evaluation code. **Period is the only intended source of variance within a (L, H) block.** Seeds are a shared, blocked factor: the seed set {0,1,2} is identical across all cells and controls data order and init, so P comparisons are seed-paired.\n\n## 4. Execution protocol (for an external machine; none possible here)\n1. V1\u2013V5; freeze a config template where only P, H, seed vary.\n2. Screening stage: run all grid cells, 3 seeds, logging per-run wall-clock and parameter count (backbone size unknown \u2014 A8).\n3. Confirmation stage: the top-2 P per (L, H) by screening mean MSE are rerun with seeds {3,4} added (n=5) to guard against screening-stage seed luck. Only confirmed means enter final conclusions.\n4. All logs/configs/results committed with the repo hash for reproducibility.\n\n## 5. Analysis plan\n- **(a) Primary readout:** per (seq_len, H), mean \u00b1 spread (SD across seeds) of test MSE and MAE as a function of P \u2014 the P-vs-error curve per horizon; overlay the 512 branch as a separate panel, never pooled with 96.\n- **(b) Pre-registered \"meaningful effect\" (fixed before running):** within a (L, H) block, declare P_a > P_b only if (i) the seed-paired mean MSE difference exceeds the pooled seed-SD across that block's cells, **and** (ii) the relative MSE reduction is \u2265 2%. Ties failing either criterion are \"no detectable difference\"; with n=3 (n=5 confirmation) no formal p-values are claimed \u2014 the threshold rule is the inference device. Noise floor cross-checked against the seed-noise control cells.\n- **(c) Interaction checks:** P\u00d7H \u2014 compare argmin-P across the four horizons per seed; report best-P(H) and declare a horizon interaction only if confirmed-stage best-P differs by \u2265 one grid step between any two horizons. P\u00d7seq_len \u2014 compare the primary-96 and secondary-512 curves; because L also changes token budget and available context, this interaction is reported as confounded-by-design (window length cannot be held fixed while changing L), interpreted qualitatively only.\n- **(d) Cycle-alignment test (gated on V1):** at L=512, test whether the confirmed optimum falls in the cycle bracket around the verified C (128\u2013288 with padding branch; 128/256 adjacency without it).\n- **(e) Discovery comparison (gated on V4):** auto-discovered period(s) vs the sweep envelope: is discovery within the pre-registered noise threshold of the sweep argmin? Report both directions (discovery suboptimal \u2192